   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "har or er?",
     "texto": [
      "Almost always **har** + participle: Jeg **har** set dem.\n\nBut **er** is used if the verb **has no direct object** and expresses **movement or change**:\n\n- Han **er** kommet hjem. (he has come home)\n- Hun **er** rejst. (she has left)\n- Han **er** blevet tysker. (he has become German)\n\nIn the past: **havde** / **var** + participle."
     ],
     "tutora": "pensando"
    },
    {
     "tipo": "tabla",
     "titulo": "Body and personal things",
     "columnas": [
      {
       "texto": "Danés",
       "lengua": "estudio"
      },
      {
       "texto": "English",
       "lengua": "alumno"
      }
     ],
     "filas": [
      [
       {
        "texto": "Pas på **hovedet**!",
        "lengua": "estudio"
       },
       {
        "texto": "Mind your head!",
        "lengua": "alumno"
       }
      ],
      [
       {
        "texto": "Jeg har fået noget i **øjet**.",
        "lengua": "estudio"
       },
       {
        "texto": "Something has got into my eye.",
        "lengua": "alumno"
       }
      ],
      [
       {
        "texto": "**Hendes** ansigt er smukt.",
        "lengua": "estudio"
       },
       {
        "texto": "Her face is pretty.",
        "lengua": "alumno"
       }
      ]
     ],
     "nota": "Unlike English, Danish uses the article («the head»), not the possessive. But if it's the subject, use the possessive.",
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Where «ikke» goes",
     "texto": [
      "After the **conjugated verb**, and before the participle or infinitive:\n\n- Hun er **ikke** kørt.\n- Skal vi **ikke** spise nu?\n- I går regnede det **ikke**."
     ],
